The '''Rimac Nevera''' (pronounced: [[Help:IPA/Serbo-Croatian|[rǐːmat͡s něʋeːra]]]) is an [[Battery electric vehicle|all-electric]] [[sports car]] designed and manufactured by the Croatian automotive manufacturer [[Rimac Automobili]].<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.nbcnews.com/business/autos/what-s-new-geneva-auto-show-croatian-hypercar-electric-porsche-n855296 |title=What's new at the Geneva Auto Show: A Croatian hypercar, an electric Porsche, and more |first=Paul A. |last=Eisenstein |work=NBC News |location=US |date=10 March 2018 |access-date=11 March 2018}}</ref> The first production prototype car was released in August 2021.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Seabaugh|first=Christian|date=24 August 2021|title=2022 Rimac Nevera First Drive: The Promise Maker|url=https://www.motortrend.com/reviews/2022-rimac-nevera-electric-hypercar-first-drive-review/|access-date=27 August 2021|website=Motortrend}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|last=Bell|first=Lucas|date=13 August 2021|title=Watch the 1900-HP Rimac Nevera Set an 8.52-Second Quarter-Mile World Record|url=https://www.roadandtrack.com/news/a37304155/rimac-nevera-quarter-mile-world-record/|access-date=18 August 2021|website=Road & Track|language=en-US}}</ref>  Nevera production has been limited to 150 vehicles.<ref name="cnet">{{cite news|last=Wong|first=Jon|date=7 March 2018|title=Rimac C Two electric hypercar shocks Geneva with 1,914 horsepower|work=CNET|location=US|url=https://www.cnet.com/roadshow/news/rimac-c-two-electric-hypercar-shocks-geneva-with-1914-horsepower/|access-date=11 March 2018}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|last=Carney|first=Dan|date=7 March 2018|title=The New 1,914-HP Electric Hypercar From Rimac Hits 60-MPH in an Insane 1.85 Seconds|work=Maxim|url=https://www.maxim.com/rides/rimac-c-two-electric-hypercar-2018-3|access-date=11 March 2018}}</ref> Having completed crash testing for [[homologation]], Rimac planned to deliver the Nevera to customers in mid-2022.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Doll |first=Scooter |date=11 April 2022 |title=Rimac shares Nevera winter testing footage ahead of deliveries to all 150 customers |url=https://electrek.co/2022/04/11/rimac-shares-nevera-winter-testing-footage-ahead-of-deliveries-to-all-150-customers/ |access-date=4 June 2022 |website=Electrek |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Bell |first=Sebastien |date=17 February 2022 |title=Rimac Completes The Final U.S. Crash Test For The Nevera Supercar |url=https://www.carscoops.com/2022/02/rimac-completes-the-final-u-s-crash-test-for-the-nevera-supercar/ |website=Car Scoops}}</ref> The first production spec Nevera was delivered in August 2022.<ref name="First production">{{cite web|url=https://www.motor1.com/news/602523/nico-rosberg-picks-up-first-production-rimac-nevera/|title=Nico Rosberg Takes Delivery Of First Production Rimac Nevera|first1=Chris|last1=Bruce|publisher=[[Motor1]]|date=4 August 2022|access-date=25 June 2023}}</ref> Deliveries to the United States started in June 2023.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.teslarati.com/rimac-nevera-double-delivery-hypercar-united-states/|title=Rimac pushes two more Nevera hypercar EVs to U.S. customers|first1=Joey|last1=Klender|publisher=Teslarati|date=19 June 2023|access-date=25 June 2023}}</ref> The Nevera is manufactured in the same factory and at the same rate (of roughly 1 per week) as the [[Pininfarina Battista]], which is based on the same platform.<ref>{{cite web|last=Gibbs|first=Nick|date=18 January 2020|title=Rimac founder pushes hypercar maker to become a Tier 1 supplier|url=https://europe.autonews.com/automakers/rimac-founder-pushes-hypercar-maker-become-tier-1-supplier|access-date=20 January 2020|website=Automotive News Europe}}</ref>

== Overview ==
[[File:Rimac C Two 20180929 DSC 8816 (cropped).jpg|thumb|Rimac C_Two]]
The car was unveiled at the [[Geneva Motor Show#2018|2018 Geneva Motor Show]] as the Rimac C_Two; it was later renamed to the Nevera upon its launch. It is the automaker's second car after the [[Rimac Concept One]]. Rimac described its development as "pursuit of the ultimate electric hypercar driving experience".

The name Nevera comes from the Croatian word for sudden and short storms, usually accompanied by lightning, which occur primarily along the Croatian [[Adriatic Sea|Adriatic]] coast.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Nevera info page on the Rimac Automobili website|url=https://www.rimac-automobili.com/nevera/|access-date=3 June 2021|website=www.rimac-automobili.com|language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title=The entry for "nevera" on Hrvatski jezični portal|url=https://hjp.znanje.hr/index.php?show=search_by_id&id=eF1lUBU%3D&keyword=nevera|access-date=2 June 2021|website=hjp.znanje.hr|language=hr}}</ref>

== Development ==
The Nevera was initially revealed as the C_Two concept car. Since 2018, Rimac spent over three years refining the car as part of an extensive test and development programme. Almost all key components of the Nevera are designed and manufactured at Rimac's headquarters near Zagreb, Croatia.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Rimac Nevera Takes the Hypercar Market by Storm|url=https://www.rimac-automobili.com/media/press-releases/rimac-nevera-takes-the-hypercar-market-by-storm/|access-date=22 June 2021|website=Rimac Automobili|language=en-US}}</ref>

During the global homologation process, the company constructed 4 prototypes for different testing purposes.<ref name="pos" />

In June 2020, Rimac opened a new facility in [[Veliko Trgovišće]] which serves as the assembly for Nevera homologation prototypes as well as production vehicles for customers. At full capacity, it was planned that the facility would produce 4 vehicles per month, including an additional 13 prototype vehicles for homologation testing to destruction and 10 pre-production vehicles, by the end of 2020.<ref name=pos/> YouTuber and automotive influencer, [[Misha Charoudin]], was amongst the first people to test the Rimac Nevera, test driving a prototype on a closed circuit track.<ref>{{Cite web |last1=Anderson |first1=Brad |title=Go For A POV Drive In The All-Electric Rimac C Two Hypercar |url=https://www.carscoops.com/2020/05/go-for-a-pov-drive-in-the-all-electric-rimac-c_two-hypercar/ |website=Car Scoops |date=21 May 2020 }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last= |first= |date=2020-05-22 |title=Elektrisches Supercar: Fahrer Verliert Bei Test Im Schnellen Rimac-Prototypen Die Fassung |url=https://teslamag.de/news/elektrisches-supercar-rimac-ctwo-testfahrer-verliert-fassung-28478 |access-date=2024-03-12 |website=Teslamag |language=de-DE}}</ref>

In February 2022, after four years of testing, the crash test program for the global homologation of the Nevera was completed, when US passive safety tests were completed. European crash testing had already been completed in 2021.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.electrichybridvehicletechnology.com/news/testing/worlds-fastest-electric-car-completes-four-year-crash-test-program.html |title='World's fastest electric car' completes four-year crash test program |first=James |last=Billington |work=Electric & Hybrid Vehicle Technology International |location=UK |date=18 February 2022 |access-date=20 February 2022}}</ref>

===Production===
In August 2022, [[2016 Formula One]] World Champion [[Nico Rosberg]] took delivery of car number 1 of a planned production run of 150.<ref>{{Cite web |date=4 August 2022 |title=Bivši prvak Formule 1 prvi je kupio Rimčevu Neveru: Jedva čekam da vidim što može! |trans-title=The former Formula 1 champion was the first to buy Rimac's Nevera: I can't wait to see what it can do! |url=https://www.tportal.hr/autozona/clanak/video-bivsi-prvak-formule-1-prvi-je-kupio-rimcevu-neveru-jedva-cekam-da-vidim-sto-moze-20220804/print |access-date=1 October 2022 |website=tportal.hr |language=hr}}</ref> In May 2024, founder and CEO [[Mate Rimac]] said more than 50 vehicles had been delivered to customers, indicating that the pace was slower than anticipated.<ref>{{cite web |url= https://www.autocar.co.uk/car-news/new-cars/rimac-unlikely-make-another-electric-hypercar |title=Rimac unlikely to make another electric hypercar |last=Page |first=Felix |date=June 19, 2024 |website=[[Autocar (magazine)|Autocar]] |access-date=June 19, 2024 |quote=}}</ref>

== Performance ==
Each of the Nevera's four wheels is independently driven by a carbon-sleeve permanent-magnet electric motor through a single-speed [[gearbox]].<ref name="Specifications"/> Combined, they produce a total of {{cvt|1914|PS|kW hp|0}} and {{cvt|2360|Nm|lbft|0}} of torque.<ref>{{cite web | last=Sutcliffe | first=Steve | title=Rimac Nevera 2023 review | website=evo | date=2022-12-16 | url=https://www.evo.co.uk/rimac/nevera | access-date=2023-08-31}}</ref><ref>{{cite web | last=Hawkins | first=Andrew J. | title=Rimac Nevera hits top speed of 256 mph to become world's fastest production EV | website=The Verge | date=2022-11-15 | url=https://www.theverge.com/2022/11/15/23460105/rimac-nevera-world-record-top-speed-ev-production | access-date=2023-08-31}}</ref><ref name="performance">{{cite AV media |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A4orCB71BgY |title=Ferrari SF90 v new Rimac Nevera: Drag Race *World Record!!!* |date=June 1, 2021 |last=Watson |first=Mat |type=Video |publisher=carwow |access-date= |via=YouTube}}</ref>

On 15 November 2022, Rimac released two videos showing the car reaching {{cvt|256|mph|km/h|0|order=flip}} on the Automotive Testing [[Papenburg]] track in [[Germany]], leading to media outlets calling it the "fastest electric car in the world".<ref name="topspeedperformance">{{cite AV media |title=Rimac Nevera Is the World's Fastest Electric Car After Hitting 256 MPH |url=https://jalopnik.com/rimac-nevera-is-the-worlds-fastest-electric-car-after-h-1849784204 |first=Andy |last=Kalmowitz |work=Jalopnik |location=US |date=15 November 2022 |access-date=18 November 2022}}</ref>

Rimac announced on May 17, 2023, that the Nevera set 23 performance records in a single day, including {{cvt|0–60|mph|km/h|0|order=flip}} in 1.74&nbsp;s, {{cvt|0–100|km/h|mph|0}} in 1.82&nbsp;s, {{cvt|1/4|mile|m|0|order=flip}} in 8.25&nbsp;s, and {{cvt|0|-|400|-|0|km/h|mph}} in 29.93&nbsp;s.<ref>{{Cite web |date=May 17, 2023 |title=Rimac Nevera Sets 23 Performance Records in a Single Day |url=https://www.rimac-newsroom.com/press-releases/rimac-automobili/rimac-nevera-sets-23-performance-records-in-a-single-day |access-date= |website=Rimac Newsroom |language=en}}</ref>

On 17 July 2023, it set the record for the fastest production EV car at the [[Goodwood Festival of Speed]], clocking at 49.32 seconds.<ref name="insideevsgwrec">{{cite web |last1=Bruce |first1=Chris |title=See Rimac Nevera Set EV Production Car Record At Goodwood Festival of Speed |url=https://insideevs.com/news/677261/rimac-nevera-sets-fastest-electric-production-car-time-at-goodwood-festival-of-speed/ |website=InsideEvs |access-date=26 July 2023}}</ref>

On 18 August 2023, it set the record for the fastest production EV car at the [[Nürburgring]], at [[list of Nürburgring Nordschleife lap times|7:05.298 minutes]], driven by Croatian driver [[Martin Kodrić]].<ref>{{Cite press release |date=2023-08-18 |title=The Rimac Nevera sets a new record at Nürburgring and celebrates with the global premiere of Nevera Time Attack: a One-of-12 |url=https://www.rimac-newsroom.com/press-releases/rimac-nevera/the-rimac-nevera-sets-a-new-record-at-nurburgring-and-celebrates-with-the-global |access-date=2023-08-19 |publisher=Rimac}}</ref>

== Features and specifications ==
While also advertising a maximum range of {{cvt|402|miles|km|0|abbr=in|order=flip}} on the [[NEDC test cycle]],<ref name=dreams>{{cite news |url=https://www.t3.com/news/the-rimac-c-two-is-the-electric-hypercar-of-your-dreams |title=The Rimac C_Two is the electric hypercar of your dreams |first=Spencer |last=Hart |work=T3 |location=UK |date=7 March 2018 |access-date=30 March 2018}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.autoblog.com/2018/03/06/rimac-c_two-electric-hypercar-almost-2-000-horsepower/ |title=Rimac C_Two is a next-gen EV hypercar from Croatia |first=John Beltz |last=Snyder |work=Autoblog |location=US |date=6 March 2018 |access-date=30 March 2018}}</ref> and {{cvt|490|km}} on the [[WLTP driving cycle]], the car is also claimed to be able to complete two consecutive laps of the [[Nürburgring]] with a negligible drop in performance.<ref>{{cite news |last=Schroeder |first=Stan |date=March 6, 2018 |title=Rimac's new supercar goes from 0–60 mph in an insane 1.74 seconds |work=[[Mashable]] |location= |url=https://mashable.com/2018/03/06/rimac-1914-hp-supercar/#w_DP1Kbs.ZqI |access-date=}}</ref> Rimac claims the car was designed to be very durable and could be driven hard. In addition to this, the car has sensors needed for [[SAE levels|SAE Level 4]] of autonomous driving (for when an upgrade has been developed enable them)<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.topgear.com/car-news/electric/new-1900bhp-rimac-ctwo-258mph-self-driving-electric-hypercar |title=The Rimac C_Two is a 258mph self-driving electric hypercar |first=Ollie |last=Kew  |work=Top Gear |location=UK |date=6 March 2018 |access-date=30 March 2018}}</ref> with full [[advanced driver-assistance system]] (ADAS).<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.roadandtrack.com/car-shows/geneva-auto-show/a19126167/rimac-c-two-1914-hp-full-specs-and-photos/ |title=Rimac C_Two: 1914 HP and a 1.85-Second 0–60 Time |first=Máté |last=Petrány |work=Road & Track |location=US |date=6 March 2018 |access-date=30 March 2018}}</ref> If connected to a [[fast charger]], it can be recharged to 80% in less than 30 minutes.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.express.co.uk/life-style/cars/929370/Rimac-Concept-Two-electric-hypercar-Tesla-Roadster-petrol-killer |title=Rimac Concept Two electric hypercar is not a Tesla Roadster killer its a PETROL killer |first=Luke John |last=Smith |work=Express |location=UK |date=9 March 2018 |access-date=30 March 2018}}</ref> The car features an entirely new design and does away with the conventional doors, now incorporating [[butterfly doors]] instead. It also has a fire extinguisher in the back held in by a leather strap embossed with the words "In case of hill climb, extinguish fire", a reference to ''[[The Grand Tour]]'' host [[Richard Hammond]] who crashed a [[Rimac Concept One|Concept One]] during a hill climb, causing it to catch on fire; Hammond survived the crash with a broken leg.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Silvestro |first=Brian |date=9 March 2018 |title=The Rimac C_Two Electric Hypercar Is Richard Hammond-Proof |work=[[Road & Track]] |url=https://www.roadandtrack.com/new-cars/a19299046/rimac-c-two-fire-extinguisher-richard-hammond/ |access-date=23 November 2019}}</ref>

== Nevera R ==
Rimac unveiled the '''Nevera R''' in 17 August 2024 as a track-focused and more powerful variation of the Nevera. Each of the four electric motors were significantly improved to produce a total output of {{cvt|2107|PS|kW hp|0}} and {{cvt|2360|Nm|lbft|0}}. The R was also lighter than the base model, weighing in approximately at {{cvt|4993|lb|kg|0|order=flip}}. This meant that it accelerates from {{cvt|0–60|mph|km/h|0|order=flip}} in 1.74 s (1.66 s in other tests), {{cvt|0–62|mph|km/h|0|order=flip}} in 1.88 s and {{cvt|0–186.5|mph|km/h|0|order=flip}} in 7.89 s, and reaches a top speed of {{cvt|268.2|mph|km/h|1|order=flip}}.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Rimac Nevera R Becomes New 0-400-0 Champion and Sets 24 Performance Records |url=https://www.rimac-newsroom.com/press-releases/rimac-automobili/rimac-nevera-r-becomes-new-0-400-0-champion-and-sets-24-performance-records |website=Rimac Newsroom}}</ref> The Nevera R features a new fixed rear wing, larger diffuser and a redesigned front bumper, increasing downforce by 15% and aero efficiency by 10%. It is also equipped with [[Michelin]] Cup 2 tyres, improved torque vectoring and a more negative camber, reducing understeer by 10% and increasing lateral grip.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Potts |first=Greg |date=17 August 2024 |title=The new 2,078bhp Rimac Nevera R will do 0-186mph in 8.66s |url=https://www.topgear.com/car-news/electric/new-2078bhp-rimac-nevera-r-will-do-0-186mph-866s |website=Top Gear}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Stocksdale |first=Joel |date=16 August 2024 |title=Rimac Nevera R goes well beyond the 2,000-horsepower mark |url=https://www.autoblog.com/features/rimac-nevera-r-reveal-pebble-beach-monterey-2000-rpm |website=Autoblog}}</ref>

=== Records ===
On 10 July 2025, Rimac set 24 world records with the Nevera R at the Automotive Testing Papenburg track in Germany, notably reaching {{cvt|0|-|400|-|0|km/h|mph}} in 25.79 s, beating the previous world record set by the [[Koenigsegg Jesko|Koenigsegg Jesko Absolut]] in 2 July 2024 with the time of 27.93 s.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Pattni |first=Vijay |date=10 July 2025 |title=Your move, Koenigsegg: Rimac Nevera R smashes new 0-400-0kmh record |url=https://www.topgear.com/car-news/supercars/your-move-koenigsegg-rimac-nevera-r-smashes-new-0-400-0kmh-record |access-date= |website=Top Gear}}</ref>

== Reception ==
Jonathan Lopez of ''Top Speed'' magazine acclaimed the Nevera stating that "it is an absolute game changer, and not just in the EV segment. Between the onboard tech and mind-boggling performance specs, this machine has the goods to take on the best of the best."<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.topspeed.com/cars/rimac-automobile/2019-rimac-c-two/ |title=2019 Rimac Concept Two News |first=Jonathon |last=Lopez |work=Top Speed |location=UK |date=2 March 2018 |access-date=28 April 2018}}</ref>

Tom Ford of ''[[Top Gear (magazine)|Top Gear]]'' tested the early prototype in March 2020. He praised the "punch out" in the corners despite the weight of the car, as well as giving plenty of feedback, concluding that "it tastes good raw, even without the torque-vectoring wizardry. ... But with a base car that shows this level of promise, and a company that focuses on fun rather than figures, this bodes well."<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.topgear.com/car-reviews/ctwo/first-drive |title=Rimac C_Two prototype review: world exclusive drive of 1,887bhp EV Reviews 2021 |first=Tom |last=Ford |work=Top Gear |location=UK |date=10 March 2020 |access-date=29 August 2021}}</ref>

Vlad Savov of ''[[The Verge]]'' criticized its looks by describing them as "anonymous and unexciting", and describing them as less flamboyant than that of [[Lamborghini Huracán]], but admitted that the car is "more forgiving and accommodating than most other hypercars", but also stated that the readouts on the [[infotainment]] were too distracting.<ref>{{cite news |last=Savov |first=Vlad |date=8 March 2018 |title=Rimac's Concept Two is a soulless speed demon you unlock with your face |work=[[The Verge]] |location= |url=https://www.theverge.com/2018/3/8/17095430/rimac-concept-two-geneva-motor-show-2018 |access-date=}}</ref>

=== Production version ===
''Top Gear'', in its 2021 review of a pre-production vehicle, praised the "head spinning performance, incredible tech, ultra-stiff chassis, engineering and build quality" but noted that the brakes need getting used to and some detail finessing, giving it 9 out of 10.<ref name="top_gear_nevera_review">{{Cite web |last=Top Gear Team |date=1 June 2021 |title=Rimac Nevera Review 2021 |url=https://www.topgear.com/car-reviews/rimac/nevera |website=[[Top Gear (magazine)|Top Gear]]}}</ref> Chris Perkins, writing for ''[[Road & Track]]'', called the acceleration "savage and unrelenting", noting that the "step up in performance between 'quite fast' and 'so fast it makes breathing difficult' is quite something", and ultimately calling it "the most advanced, most powerful, quickest car out there".<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.roadandtrack.com/news/a36560460/rimac-nevera-first-drive-ev-hypercar/|title=The Rimac Nevera Redefines Speed|date=June 2021}}</ref> ''[[Car and Driver]]'' had similar impressions in its review, stating that "hypercars like the Nevera aren't for everyone, but there's no denying its significance as the moment a battery-powered car toppled the [[Bugatti Chiron]]. The internal-combustion engine may never catch up".<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.caranddriver.com/reviews/a36607982/2021-rimac-nevera-drive/|title = 2021 Rimac Nevera May Upend the Hypercar Paradigm|date = 7 June 2021}}</ref> ''[[Robb Report]]'' journalist Ben Oliver noted in his impressions while driving the car that "the noise adds to the drama, as much psychological as physical, in a way no other road car can match, making for a dangerously charismatic split personality worth every one of its seven figures".<ref>{{cite web |last=Oliver |first=Ben |date=28 August 2021 |title=What It's Like to Drive the Rimac Nevera EV, the World's Fastest-Accelerating Production Car |url=https://robbreport.com/motors/cars/rimac-nevera-driving-behind-the-wheel-1234632146/ |website=[[Robb Report]]}}</ref>

